Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.470 align:start position:0%
 
drug use death threats and secret  identities  maslerman's elvis presley biopic takes a  few liberties with the truth and leaves  a few major things out here's everything  elvis gets wrong about elvis  as the film begins elvis has already cut  his first hit record that's alright mama  but it's his appearance on the  syndicated country music radio show  louisiana hayride that makes him a star  and brings him to the attention of  former carnival barker turned music  promoter colonel tom parker the  headliner in hayride is longtime country  star hank snow who is aghast at the way  presley gyrates in front of the crowd  later when parker convinces elvis to  strike out on his own snow seems all too  eager to see the young man gone snow's  reaction works as a symbol of how the  musical establishment of the day was  threatened by pressly and rock and roll  in general however in real life snow was  no prude both he and parker saw the  money-making potential in elvis early on  and the two even co-managed presley's  career for a short time  as the son jimmy roger snow noted years  later snow had every financial reason to  make elvis as successful as possible  even allowing elvis to close out their  shows snow's exit from the elvis presley  story in real life came at the hands of  parker who used snow to sweet talk  presley's family into signing a new  contract before cheating snow out of his  share as biographer james dickerson told  the new york post in june 2022 hank snow  said that parker was the lowest human  being that had ever walked the earth  the contract that in real life cheated  hank snow out of managing presley's  career is presented as a kind of deal  with the devil in the film presley's  manager colonel parker is seen pouring  honey into the ears of elvis parents  gladys and vernon to convince them to  sign at this point parker has already  mastermind presley's move from the  regional sun records to major label rca  victor and he solidifies his control  over the entire family by having them  sign into existence a new business  called elvis presley enterprises elvis  father vernon who had been jailed for  check forgery when his son was a boy  would be his business manager gladys is  skeptical of the colonel and would  remain so up until her death in 1958.  ultimately she signs a contract anyway  when parker coos about elvis having the  strength of two men a knowing reference  to elvis stillborn twin brother jessie  parker did indeed have elvis and his  family under contract with vernon  serving as the manager of elvis personal  business but the business venture known  as elvis presley enterprises or epe  wouldn't be formed until after presley's  death epe was founded by vernon the  presley's longtime accountant joseph  hanks and elvis ex-wife priscilla  presley as a way to preserve and manage  his estate  one of the most controversial aspects of  presley's life and legacy is his  relationship with blues music and  southern african-american culture in  general baz luhrmann's film suggests  that elvis use of black music and  culture was appreciation not  appropriation the movie works hard to  convey that the musician discovered  blues and gospels sincerely with  flashbacks showing a young elvis in a  black pentecostal church in mississippi  meanwhile the scenes of elvis's early  days in memphis show him on beale street  surrounded by the sounds of little  richard sister rosetta tharp and big  mama thornton whose hit song hound dog  would become one of elvis's signature  tombs the film also gives elvis a  friendship with blues legend bb king  already a well-known musician at the  time who views presley's fame with a  certain riot attachment however  according to biographer alana nash this  relationship seems to have been  extrapolated from a famous photograph of  the two men backstage at a memphis  charity show presley did not play at the  show but appeared briefly on stage and  gave the crowd a little leg shake in  real life king appears to have been fond  of pressly on a personal level if  ambivalent about the success elvis and  other white artists had adapting black  music  um elvis was very shy when i first met  him but there is no evidence that the  close friendship portrayed in the film  ever existed in real life  in just a few short years at the end of  the 1950s presley went from  transgressive provocateur to clean-cut  all-american family entertainer in the  film this is orchestrated by colonel  parker against elvis will starting with  a humiliating performance on the steve  allen show where he performs hound dog  with an actual dog on stage  i got you a very cute little hound dog  right here  [Music]  protesters lined the streets outside  graceland waving signs that read don't  change elvis and we want the old elvis  back at a pivotal show at the memphis  fairgrounds after being warned against  wiggling by both the memphis police and  his manager elvis's performance is so  intense that it causes a riot elvis is  taken away in a police car and is  threatened with jail or a stint in the  army while the steve allen performance  is based on a true story the memphis  fairground riots is a composite of  several different concerts most notably  during one show in jacksonville florida  a local judge warned presley against any  lewd behavior but the film gives the  impression that parker altered elvis  image under the threat of blackmail from  shadowy establishment forces which is  untrue biographer alana nash told  variety parker wanted to make elvis  clean cut after he came back from the  army in part because when elvis went  into the army neither elvis nor parker  thought rock and roll would last elvis  especially thought that it might even be  over by the time he got out  from 1956 to 1969 elvis presley starred  in over 30 hollywood movies his film  work dominated his career for the bulk  of the 60s which baslerman glides over  via a zippy montage the film seems to  believe that elvis hollywood years were  filled with hokey kids that bears little  resemblance to the transgressive  inherently political artists he was at  heart in 1968 however his conscience is  stirred by the assassinations of dr  martin luther king jr in april and  robert f kennedy in june after king's  assassination in memphis priscilla  suggests that presley should release a  statement telling him that memphis is  burning  while king's murder sparked riots and  violence in major cities across the  country the public reaction in memphis  was more muted hampton sides wrote about  king's murderer james earl ray in the  2010 book hellhound on his trail sides  explains that the first night in memphis  after the murder had isolated incidents  of violence and looting but where the  riots in other cities lasted nearly two  weeks after king's death memphis fell  into an eerie calm or a deep mourning  instead  in the movie elvis is in the midst of  filming a television comeback special  when robert kennedy's assassination  occurs in june 1968. colonel tom parker  has explicitly sold the show to nbc as a  christmas special to be aired in  december however elvis wants to use the  special to get back to his rock and roll  roots and chart a new way forward in his  career against the colonel's wishes he  ditches the christmas sweaters for a  black leather jumpsuit the resulting  concert film commonly known as the 68  comeback special is a smash hit and  latter day triumph for the  king there's been a big change in the  music fee in the last 10 or 12 years  however presley and parker's clash is  largely invented for the film in real  life the special was never meant to be a  christmas special other than by virtue  of aryan in december biographer elena  nash told variety there was never a  christmas sweater or fairy tale  christmas set that's lerman being a  showman  in the movie after the success of the  1968 comeback special pressly is writing  high and dreaming big he announces plans  to finally tour the world but first  parker books him into a weeks-long gig  at the brand new hilton international  hotel in las vegas the vegas residency  is a huge hit and gets extended presley  continues to plan his global tour but  parker has grave concerns about securing  elvis safety overseas especially when  startling death threats start showing up  in the mail the real reason parker is  delaying the start of the international  tour is because he's an illegal  immigrant and fears being deported if he  ever leaves the country the film heavily  suggests that a slew of death threats  received by pressly were staged by  parker in order to support his bogus  security concerns about traveling abroad  are you ready to fly  we may never know if some of those death  threats were forgeries by colonel tom  but thanks to the fbi we know that many  of them were very real from his earliest  days on the national scene the fbi kept  careful tabs on elvis and his activities  as director j edgar hoover feared that  he might be a corrupting influence on  the nation's youth the fbi's extensive  file on pressly contains a death threat  sent through the mail in 1956 as well as  confirmations of threats made to presley  and his associates in the 1970s  for decades colonel tom parker claimed  to be a good old boy from huntington  west virginia who ran off to join the  circus as a youth which is where he  picked up an ever so slight european  accent despite making enemies left and  right in the music business no one  seemed to mind where this carnival  barker came from as long as the money  kept rolling in the movie elvis is told  from parker's perspective and waited by  the revelations about his life that only  came to light after presley's death it  wasn't until the early 1980s that it  became widely known that parker was  actually andreas cornelius van kuk a  dutch immigrant who had briefly served  in the us army but had never become a  naturalized citizen he fled from the  netherlands in 1929 possibly after being  implicated in a murder and he still had  family in europe that he had broken all  contact with the filmmakers know all of  these things and assume that the  audience does as well which is perhaps  why tom hanks affects a thick dutch  accent throughout the movie  we are the same you and i  we are two odd lonely children  in the film things finally come to a  head between presley and parker in 1974.  an anonymous letter reveals the truth  about parker from his illegal status to  the fact that he essentially sold elvis  to the international hotel in exchange  for unlimited credit on the casino floor  and overcome elvis collapses backstage  before show and is given a shot of  amphetamines to revive him at parker's  command once on stage the singer fires  parker in full view of the audience  however this never happened in real life  with writer alana nash telling variety  elvis would never have been so crass as  to a fired colonel from the stage  the extent to presley's addiction to  prescription drugs wouldn't come to  light until after his untimely death at  the age of 42 of a heart attack that was  likely caused by his persistent  substance abuse he had a reliance on  amphetamines or uppers to keep up with  his daunting tour schedule and  barbiturates or downers to help him  sleep especially in the last decade of  his life while it's never been confirmed  exactly how early in his career the drug  use began the movie establishes that  presley's pill habit began on the  singer's very first backwoods tour in  1955. in 1979 the new york times quoted  anonymous sources speculating that  presley's pill habit began in the 1950s  possibly due to pressley's religious  aversion to alcohol as a result the  musician allegedly felt that  prescription drugs were more legitimate  than street narcotics another rumor is  that he was snatching his mother's diet  pills long before that  according to presley biographer peter  geraldneck it was a fellow soldier who  introduced elvis to amphetamines during  his two-year stint in the army from 1958  to 1960. the truth about when elvis's  drug use really started remains unknown  if you or anyone you know is struggling  with addiction issues help is available  visit the substance abuse and mental  health services administration website  or contact samhsa's national helpline at  1-800-662-help  4350  [Applause]  [Music]

