finally tour the world but first  parker books him into a weeks-long gig  at the brand new hilton international  hotel in las vegas the vegas residency  is a huge hit and gets extended presley  continues to plan his global tour but  parker has grave concerns about securing  elvis safety overseas especially when  startling death threats start showing up  in the mail the real reason parker is  delaying the start of the international  tour is because he's an illegal  immigrant and fears being deported if he  ever leaves the country the film heavily  suggests that a slew of death threats  received by pressly were staged by  parker in order to support his bogus  security concerns about traveling abroad  are you ready to fly  we may never know if some of those death  threats were forgeries by colonel tom  but thanks to the fbi we know that many  of them were very real from his earliest  days on the national scene the fbi kept  careful tabs on elvis and his activities  as director j edgar hoover feared that  he might be a corrupting influence on  the nation's youth the fbi's extensive  file on pressly contains a death threat  sent through the mail in 1956 as well as  confirmations of threats made to presley  and his associates in the 1970s  for decades colonel tom parker claimed  to be a good old boy from huntington  west virginia who ran off to join the  circus as a youth which is where he  picked up an ever so slight european  accent despite making enemies left and  right in the music business no one  seemed to mind where this carnival  barker came from as long as the money  kept rolling in the movie elvis is told  from parker's perspective and waited by  the revelations about his life that only  came to light after presley's death it  wasn't until the early 1980s that it  became widely known that parker was  actually andreas cornelius van kuk a  dutch immigrant who had briefly served  in the us army but had never become a  naturalized citizen he fled from the  netherlands in 1929 possibly after being  implicated in a murder and he still had  family in europe that he had broken all  contact with the filmmakers know all of  these things and assume that the  audience does as well which is perhaps  why tom hanks affects a thick dutch  accent throughout the movie  we are the same you and i  we are two odd lonely children  in the film things finally come to a  head between presley and parker in 1974.  an anonymous letter reveals the truth  about parker from his illegal status to  the fact that he essentially sold elvis  to the international hotel in exchange  for unlimited credit on the casino floor  and overcome elvis collapses backstage  before show and is given a shot of  amphetamines to revive him at parker's  command once on stage the singer fires  parker in full view of the audience  however this never happened in real life  with writer alana nash telling variety  elvis would never have been so crass as  to a fired colonel from the stage  the extent to presley's addiction to  prescription drugs wouldn't come to  light until after his untimely death at  the age of 42 of a heart attack that was  likely caused by his persistent  substance abuse he had a reliance on  amphetamines or uppers to keep up with  his daunting tour schedule and  barbiturates or downers to help him  sleep especially in the last decade of  his life while it's never been confirmed  exactly how early in his career the drug  use began the movie establishes that  presley's pill habit began on the  singer's very first backwoods tour in  1955. in 1979 the new york times quoted  anonymous sources speculating that  presley's pill habit began in the 1950s  possibly due to pressley's religious  aversion to alcohol as a result the  musician allegedly felt that  prescription drugs were more legitimate  than street narcotics another rumor is  that he was snatching his mother's diet  pills long before that  according to presley biographer peter  geraldneck it was a fellow soldier who  introduced elvis to amphetamines during  his two-year stint in the army from 1958  to 1960. the truth about when elvis's  drug use really started remains unknown  if you or anyone you know is struggling  with addiction issues help is available  visit the substance abuse and mental  health services administration website  or contact samhsa's national helpline at  1-800-662-help  4350  [Applause]  [Music]


